WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:00.990
Instructor: All right.

00:00.990 --> 00:02.190
So we have one more step

00:02.190 --> 00:04.230
to get our code up on GitHub.

00:04.230 --> 00:06.060
We just need to push it up.

00:06.060 --> 00:08.610
So if you remember option one,

00:08.610 --> 00:10.050
what we're following here,

00:10.050 --> 00:11.850
option one out of two

00:11.850 --> 00:15.030
is to create a new repo on GitHub, which we've done.

00:15.030 --> 00:16.530
Connect the local repo

00:16.530 --> 00:18.810
by adding a remote to our GitHub repo.

00:18.810 --> 00:21.060
We just did that in the previous video.

00:21.060 --> 00:23.340
Now we need to push our changes up.

00:23.340 --> 00:25.230
So we have our existing repo.

00:25.230 --> 00:29.940
I'm doing that stupid "Great Gatsby" novel, fake repo.

00:29.940 --> 00:32.460
I created an empty GitHub repo

00:32.460 --> 00:35.460
and then I set my remote called origin

00:35.460 --> 00:38.670
so that it points at this empty GitHub repo.

00:38.670 --> 00:41.940
So the final step is to push up.

00:41.940 --> 00:45.030
So we do this using the git push command.

00:45.030 --> 00:47.280
Again, this is not GitHub specific,

00:47.280 --> 00:51.630
though everything we'll be pushing to will be a GitHub repo.

00:51.630 --> 00:53.760
But the git push command is a git command

00:53.760 --> 00:56.207
that existed long before GitHub.

00:56.207 --> 00:57.540
And there are many other places

00:57.540 --> 01:00.360
where you can have a hosted repo that you can push to.

01:00.360 --> 01:02.760
So it works with any remote.

01:02.760 --> 01:06.960
Basically, the syntax is git push and then a remote.

01:06.960 --> 01:09.660
In our case, we just have one which is origin

01:09.660 --> 01:11.100
but we can have multiple.

01:11.100 --> 01:13.560
So we have to tell git where we're pushing,

01:13.560 --> 01:18.300
what's the destination repository and what branch to push.

01:18.300 --> 01:20.700
So, you know, we might have a really large repo

01:20.700 --> 01:22.050
with a ton of branches.

01:22.050 --> 01:23.130
Like, actually,

01:23.130 --> 01:25.563
we don't have a ton here but we've got a couple.

01:27.300 --> 01:28.830
No, just two. (laughs)

01:28.830 --> 01:31.650
Oh, well, this is because this video

01:31.650 --> 01:34.440
or this repo was created before we went into branching.

01:34.440 --> 01:36.150
But some of our repos have, like,

01:36.150 --> 01:38.670
I don't know, seven, eight, maybe 10 branches.

01:38.670 --> 01:41.610
And you may not want all of them on GitHub

01:41.610 --> 01:43.920
or you may not wanna push them all at once at least.

01:43.920 --> 01:45.210
So when we push,

01:45.210 --> 01:46.860
we call out a particular branch

01:46.860 --> 01:49.110
that we want to send to GitHub.

01:49.110 --> 01:51.960
Often, that will be your master or main branch.

01:51.960 --> 01:54.780
But you can push any branch up to GitHub

01:54.780 --> 01:55.890
and I'll show you that.

01:55.890 --> 01:58.350
So this is a really common command,

01:58.350 --> 02:00.900
git push origin master.

02:00.900 --> 02:03.240
Though it is becoming slightly less common

02:03.240 --> 02:06.660
as more people rename their master branch to main.

02:06.660 --> 02:07.493
If you recall,

02:07.493 --> 02:10.020
we talked about this early on when I introduced branching.

02:10.020 --> 02:14.100
It now comes up again, master versus main,

02:14.100 --> 02:15.360
because GitHub,

02:15.360 --> 02:17.910
as I mentioned at the end of 2020,

02:17.910 --> 02:21.630
decided to make the default branch name on a GitHub repo

02:21.630 --> 02:23.490
main instead of master.

02:23.490 --> 02:25.200
So their instructions tell you

02:25.200 --> 02:27.360
to add a remote called origin

02:27.360 --> 02:29.760
but then rename your branch,

02:29.760 --> 02:34.470
the master branch to main and then push main.

02:34.470 --> 02:37.620
So I will do this as well in a separate video.

02:37.620 --> 02:39.000
I will show this process.

02:39.000 --> 02:40.710
It's the exact same process.

02:40.710 --> 02:42.810
We rename a branch, we name it main

02:42.810 --> 02:44.253
and then we push up main.

02:45.270 --> 02:46.530
But I'm gonna start with master,

02:46.530 --> 02:48.210
just to keep this as simple as possible,

02:48.210 --> 02:51.030
because we have a master branch, right?

02:51.030 --> 02:53.040
We've been working with the term master,

02:53.040 --> 02:55.140
so we'll start there first.

02:55.140 --> 02:58.470
Okay, so I have this remote called origin,

02:58.470 --> 03:01.620
as we already discussed, git remote dash V.

03:01.620 --> 03:03.810
We see what it's pointing to.

03:03.810 --> 03:06.180
And it's always a good idea to see,

03:06.180 --> 03:07.830
you know, what we have,

03:07.830 --> 03:10.710
if we have any uncommitted changes that won't go up.

03:10.710 --> 03:13.050
Otherwise, we're ready.

03:13.050 --> 03:15.270
We're gonna do a git push.

03:15.270 --> 03:17.760
And then origin is where we're pushing to.

03:17.760 --> 03:21.990
And then master is the branch we are pushing up.

03:21.990 --> 03:24.210
Okay, so this is what our GitHub looks like right now.

03:24.210 --> 03:25.043
I'll refresh.

03:25.043 --> 03:27.570
There's nothing here except these instructions.

03:27.570 --> 03:28.890
This is the repo.

03:28.890 --> 03:29.910
But now I'm gonna push

03:29.910 --> 03:33.450
and there's going to be a substantial change.

03:33.450 --> 03:34.560
And remember,

03:34.560 --> 03:37.740
oh, gosh, I put images in this repo, didn't I?

03:37.740 --> 03:39.930
Well, that's why it's taking a little bit. (laughs)

03:39.930 --> 03:43.950
As you can see here, I have some images in that mood board.

03:43.950 --> 03:46.380
Usually, you don't include images in a,

03:46.380 --> 03:48.930
or at least not large images like I have

03:48.930 --> 03:50.550
but it finished up.

03:50.550 --> 03:54.693
Most repos are gonna push way, way, way faster than that,

03:55.650 --> 03:57.420
because they're usually just code.

03:57.420 --> 03:59.373
So I'm gonna refresh the page.

04:00.960 --> 04:04.110
And now we see something entirely different, right?

04:04.110 --> 04:05.730
We don't see those instructions.

04:05.730 --> 04:09.030
Now we see our file contents.

04:09.030 --> 04:11.430
So chapter one, chapter two, chapter three,

04:11.430 --> 04:13.740
characters, outline.

04:13.740 --> 04:15.810
We see different commits.

04:15.810 --> 04:17.340
I'll actually do a separate video

04:17.340 --> 04:19.650
where I walk through what we see.

04:19.650 --> 04:22.770
But we also have access to things like contributors.

04:22.770 --> 04:24.690
We can see there's only one branch,

04:24.690 --> 04:26.133
which is the master branch.

04:26.970 --> 04:30.090
And I can take a look at chapter one, for example.

04:30.090 --> 04:31.840
Here's the contents of chapter one.

04:33.180 --> 04:36.270
Okay, so we have our code up here.

04:36.270 --> 04:37.890
Now what I wanna show you

04:37.890 --> 04:40.320
is that we can push any branch, right?

04:40.320 --> 04:42.960
I talked about pushing master,

04:42.960 --> 04:44.760
because that's what we have on this project.

04:44.760 --> 04:48.330
But if we make a new branch, which I'll just make,

04:48.330 --> 04:49.830
what should my branch be called?

04:49.830 --> 04:52.503
How about empty or nearly empty?

04:53.430 --> 04:55.200
I switched to that branch

04:55.200 --> 04:58.560
